    {
      "id": 17,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"synthesis\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Focus on the evidence-backed sort improvement: replace per-column bubble sort with a stable bottom-up merge sort using one reusable scratch buffer, and scan sorted runs for the mode. Preserve tie and floating-point behavior, handle NaNs explicitly, and use safe flattened offsets. This approach measured 0.0053166 s, the best observed result.\",\"B\":\"Explore an alternative that avoids fully sorting every column: compute the median with robust order-statistic selection and the mode with exact frequency tracking. Match the sorted-scan tie rule (highest frequency, then smallest value), preserve signed-zero behavior, and use a reference-compatible fallback for cases the method cannot safely handle. Benchmark it against the merge-sort candidate; keep it only if correctness and measured time improve.\",\"C\":\"Investigate OpenMP only as a workload-gated option: columns are independent, so test one static parallel loop over columns with a sufficiently high threshold and no nested parallel regions. The observed OpenMP variants were slower (about 0.0070–0.0083 s versus 0.0053 s for the best sequential candidate), so do not assume parallelism helps or integrate it without a measured win on the actual workload.\",\"G\":\"Use the 0.0053166 s merge-sort candidate as the integration baseline, then compare complete candidate programs under the same correctness and timing harness. Treat selection/frequency tracking and parallelism as alternatives to validate, not additive gains; retain only changes that preserve outputs for valid inputs and improve measured end-to-end time.\",\"analysis\":\"The source is Problem B’s column-wise descriptive statistics. The baseline measured 0.0092942 s. The two A merge-sort candidates measured 0.0053166 s and 0.0053590 s; the observed OpenMP candidates measured 0.0069973–0.0083164 s. This points to replacing quadratic bubble sorting as the strongest demonstrated direction, while the timings caution against parallel overhead. Keep the official metric and output semantics, and do not specialize to the observed test cases.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"4fb22ff19d009f208425d5338e3edcf8f3d6f5e8957807aceec5d3d8b015db75\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Focus on the evidence-backed sort improvement: replace per-column bubble sort with a stable bottom-up merge sort using one reusable scratch buffer, and scan sorted runs for the mode. Preserve tie and floating-point behavior, handle NaNs explicitly, and use safe flattened offsets. This approach measured 0.0053166 s, the best observed result.\",\"B\":\"Explore an alternative that avoids fully sorting every column: compute the median with robust order-statistic selection and the mode with exact frequency tracking. Match the sorted-scan tie rule (highest frequency, then smallest value), preserve signed-zero behavior, and use a reference-compatible fallback for cases the method cannot safely handle. Benchmark it against the merge-sort candidate; keep it only if correctness and measured time improve.\",\"C\":\"Investigate OpenMP only as a workload-gated option: columns are independent, so test one static parallel loop over columns with a sufficiently high threshold and no nested parallel regions. The observed OpenMP variants were slower (about 0.0070–0.0083 s versus 0.0053 s for the best sequential candidate), so do not assume parallelism helps or integrate it without a measured win on the actual workload.\",\"G\":\"Use the 0.0053166 s merge-sort candidate as the integration baseline, then compare complete candidate programs under the same correctness and timing harness. Treat selection/frequency tracking and parallelism as alternatives to validate, not additive gains; retain only changes that preserve outputs for valid inputs and improve measured end-to-end time.\",\"analysis\":\"The source is Problem B’s column-wise descriptive statistics. The baseline measured 0.0092942 s. The two A merge-sort candidates measured 0.0053166 s and 0.0053590 s; the observed OpenMP candidates measured 0.0069973–0.0083164 s. This points to replacing quadratic bubble sorting as the strongest demonstrated direction, while the timings caution against parallel overhead. Keep the official metric and output semantics, and do not specialize to the observed test cases.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"4d3fc123f5eb4cab90db05b212dd3580\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"4fb22ff19d009f208425d5338e3edcf8f3d6f5e8957807aceec5d3d8b015db75\",\"session_id\":\"c08531b8446a447bacb06c4500c0c033\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"65bcbe891cf79c0bc4b8e0dbc473ab3f6fe283d8430d2843194aa002f1c75218\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":22573,\"output_tokens\":5566,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":5140}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":28139,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
